}
<time datetime="2023-02-26">February 26, 2023</time>
<article id="Markup" class="WebBits">
    <h2>TRY: Finding markup differences</h2>
    <p>
        JavaScript requires <abbr title="HyperText Markup Language">HTML</abbr> to load before the JavaScript code.
        Developers often choose to preload their websites with small amounts of html, allowing JavaScript to fill in
        after. This results in different source code than what is applied during browsing.
    </p>
    <!--image comparison-->
    <figure id="twoImgFig">
        <div id="comparison">
            <figure>
                <div id="divisor"></div>
            </figure>
            <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" id="slider">
        </div>
        <figcaption>
            <strong>Left:</strong> source code.
            <strong>Right:</strong> inspected code.
        </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
        Blazor, Angular, and React web application frameworks are based on this concept. Each app begins its page
        render with a seeded html source code then fills in the ramining markup as it's needed. This is known as
        dynamic rendering.
    </p>
    <aside class="exampleBlue">
        <ul>
            <li class="noindent">
                <kbd><kbd>CTRL</kbd>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<kbd>U</kbd></kbd> to open source code.
            </li>
            <li class="noindent">
                <kbd><kbd>CTRL</kbd>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<kbd>SHIFT</kbd>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<kbd>I</kbd></kbd> to open elements
                inspection
            </li>
        </ul>
    </aside>
</article>
<acronyms-list></acronyms-list>
